                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "So I only played this for a good 15 minutes roughly (to the 3rd level), because I just couldn't force myself to play any further. The concept is really good, and I really liked the idea of mixing different people at different tables for different bonuses (and occasional negatives). That was a nice, fresh mechanic that I'd like to see more of. The graphics are great for this kind of game, the music is alright and the sound effects are good. \n\nMy problem is how clunky it is. I really dislike that I have to drag patrons over to their chair. Dragging patrons over to their seat is hit or miss - you can be as accurate as possible but it still won't work. At first I thought this was because the previous patron stands next to the chair after paying for a second (which really should not stop you seating anyone), but that doesn't seem to be the case. You can queue up actions, but there's no point in doing that since you're never sure when a patron is going to mess up the table, or ask for a snack. The second they do, consider the rest of the level screwed because you'll be late on everything following.\n\nThe tutorial guides you to buy the first upgrade, but it's very very likely that you won't have enough money to do so from the first level if you made a single mistake. It also doesn't even bother explaining the extra snacks they might ask for. Everything goes so so so slowly. The patience indicator obviously goes down bit by bit but there's no way to tell that it's about to happen - and there's no way to recover when it does (say, by being quick on everything else).\n\nThere's potential here, for sure, but it's like the developers looked at all the things other games in this genre did right and ignored them. And then added a few unnecessary mechanics to make things worse. Maybe it gets better in later levels, but it really wasn't worth continuing to find out.",
